From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 06:25:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] PackageManager: let an unowned preinstalled package adopt its
 sysconfig update owner

A sysconfig <update-ownership> entry only ever latched on a clean
install. InstallPackageHelper, scanning a preinstalled system package:

    final boolean isSameUpdateOwner = isUpdateOwnershipEnabled
            && TextUtils.equals(oldUpdateOwner, updateOwnerFromSysconfig);

    if (!pkgAlreadyExists || isSameUpdateOwner) {
        pkgSetting.setUpdateOwnerPackage(updateOwnerFromSysconfig);
    } else {
        pkgSetting.setUpdateOwnerPackage(null);
    }

isUpdateOwnershipEnabled is `oldUpdateOwner != null`. On an upgrade the
package already exists and its recorded owner is null, so
isSameUpdateOwner is false and the else branch runs, clearing the owner
on every boot. An entry added to the image after a device first installed
the app can therefore never take effect.

That is the normal case for a ROM that ships preinstalled F-Droid builds
and gains the entry later: every dirty-flashed device keeps a null owner,
and because these APKs carry upstream's own certificate, the Play Store
sees a package it recognises with a matching signature and takes over its
updates. Measured on a Pixel 3a XL: eleven bundled apps, all of them
shipping an entry, not one owner set.

Adopt the sysconfig owner when the recorded owner is null as well. Narrow
on purpose: the clearing rule exists so a system image cannot take a
package away from the installer the user chose, and nothing is being
taken when nothing owned it. A real owner that disagrees with sysconfig,
including the Play Store after a deliberate takeover, is still cleared.

---
 .../com/android/server/pm/InstallPackageHelper.java  | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/InstallPackageHelper.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/InstallPackageHelper.java
index d23c5d943ff4..b9ccbb355e6e 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/InstallPackageHelper.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/InstallPackageHelper.java
@@ -421,12 +421,22 @@ final class InstallPackageHelper {
             final boolean isSameUpdateOwner = isUpdateOwnershipEnabled
                     && TextUtils.equals(oldUpdateOwner, updateOwnerFromSysconfig);
 
+            // lineage-24.0: a preinstalled package that nothing has ever owned adopts the
+            // sysconfig owner too, not just one being installed for the first time. Without this
+            // the entry only ever latches on a clean install: on an upgrade the package already
+            // exists, its recorded owner is null, so isSameUpdateOwner is false and the branch
+            // below actively clears the owner on every boot. An update-ownership entry added after
+            // a device first installed the app therefore never takes effect, and Play silently
+            // takes the app over on every dirty-flashed device. Narrow on purpose. The clearing
+            // rule exists so a system image cannot steal a package from the installer the user
+            // chose, and nothing is being stolen when the recorded owner is null.
+            //
             // Here we handle the update owner for the system package, and the rules are:
             // -. We use the update owner from sysconfig as the initial value.
             // -. Once an app becomes to system app later via OTA, only retains the update
             //    owner if it's consistence with sysconfig.
             // -. Clear the update owner when update owner changes from sysconfig.
-            if (!pkgAlreadyExists || isSameUpdateOwner) {
+            if (!pkgAlreadyExists || isSameUpdateOwner || oldUpdateOwner == null) {
                 pkgSetting.setUpdateOwnerPackage(updateOwnerFromSysconfig);
             } else {
                 pkgSetting.setUpdateOwnerPackage(null);
